  "response": "The feature that allows a filtered nearest-neighbor query on a pgvector approximate index to continue scanning until enough rows are found is called \"iterative index scans.\" This feature, available starting from version 0.8.0 of pgvector, enables the index to automatically scan more of the index until the desired number of results are found or a maximum limit is reached, such as `hnsw.max_scan_tuples` or `ivfflat.max_probes`.\n\nThere are two modes for iterative scans:\n\n1. **Strict Ordering**: Ensures results are returned in the exact order by distance.\n2. **Relaxed Ordering**: Allows results to be slightly out of order by distance but provides better recall.\n\nTo enable iterative scans, you can set the `iterative_scan` parameter in PostgreSQL:\n\n- For strict ordering:\n  ```sql\n  SET hnsw.iterative_scan = strict_order;\n  ```\n\n- For relaxed ordering:\n  ```sql\n  SET hnsw.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;\n  ```\n  or\n  ```sql\n  SET ivfflat.iterative_scan = relaxed_order;\n  ```\n\nThis feature is particularly useful when using approximate indexes, which might initially return fewer results than the specified `LIMIT` due to their approximate nature.",
